# PR(1)

## NAME

pr - print file

## SYNOPSIS

| **pr** [ option ] ... [ file ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Pr* produces a printed listing of one or more *files.* The output is separated into pages
headed by a date, the name of the file or a specified header, and the page number. If there are
no file arguments, *pr* prints its standard input.

Options apply to all following files but may be reset between files:

**-**\&*n*
: Produce *n*-column output. *Note: n* must be between 1 and 72 and no larger than the page
  width.

**+**\&*n*
: Begin printing with page *n.*

**-h**
: Take the next argument as a page header.

**-w**\&*n*
: For purposes of multi-column output, take the width of the page to be *n* characters instead of
  the default 72.

**-l**\&*n*
: Take the length of the page to be *n* lines instead of the default 66. *Note:* without **-t**,
  a length of 10 or less is silently taken as 66, ten lines being what the heading and the
  trailer need between them.

**-t**
: Do not print the 5-line header or the 5-line trailer normally supplied for each page.

**-s**\&*c*
: Separate columns by the single character *c* instead of by the appropriate amount of white
  space. A missing *c* is taken to be a tab.

**-m**
: Print all *files* simultaneously, each in one column,

Inter-terminal messages via write(1) are forbidden during a *pr*. *Note:* neither write(1) nor
mesg(1) is on this system yet - see *cmd/TODO.md* - but *pr* still sets the mode of its own
terminal to 0600 for the duration of a listing and puts it back on the way out, so that they
need no change when they arrive.

## HOW A PAGE IS LAID OUT

A page is *length* lines whether or not there is that much input: the heading is five lines,
the body is *length*-5 lines padded out with blanks, and the trailer is the five lines left
over. Under **-t** the body alone is *length* lines, and it is still padded.

Each column of an *n*-column listing holds *length*-10 lines - *length* under **-t** - so
**-3** on a default page is three columns of 56 lines, and column *k* begins 56\&*k* lines into
the file. Every column but the last is truncated at *width*/*n* characters; the last one, and
single-column output, are never truncated.

## COLUMNS ARE COUNTED IN BYTES

*Note:* a character above U+007F occupies more than one byte, and *pr* counts each of them as a
column. So a Cyrillic column aligns against an ASCII one by byte and not by letter. This is the
same limitation col(1) has, and the same one the shell's pattern language has, where **?**
matches one byte.

## FILES

/dev/tty? to suspend messages.

## SEE ALSO

cat(1), col(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

There are no diagnostics when *pr* is printing on a terminal.

**pr: too many columns for the buffer** - *pr* reads ahead so that every column of a page can
be filled in one pass over the input, and it holds that look-ahead in a single 6720-byte
circular buffer. More columns, or a longer page, than the buffer can carry is refused. *Note:*
v7 did not check this. It silently began the affected column part way down the file instead,
with no diagnostic of any kind, so that **pr -l800 -2** over a 24-kilobyte file started its
first column 840 lines late.

## BUGS

*Pr* exits 0 after every one of its diagnostics, so a shell script cannot tell a refused
listing from a printed one. That is v7's behaviour and is left alone.
